Title: Nick and Me
Credit: Written by
Author: Anonymous
Source: Adapted from the book Nick and Me
Draft date: 3/21/2025
Notes: Photorealistic live-action feature. Narrator's given name is withheld on-screen until the final beat. Midwestern working-class period from late 1970s childhood through late 1990s into 2000s Seattle.

===

FADE IN:

EXT. KIRK STREET - MILWAUKEE - DAY

A big brick fifth-floor walk-up. Cold Midwest light. Down the block, a no-frills gym sign: MOXIE'S.

(SOUND: distant traffic, wind off the lake)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Big brother was mine. Not that stupid book. My real Big Brother.

INT. MOXIE'S GYM - DAY

NICK, tall, heavily muscled, reddish-brown hair uncombed, scars on chin and over left eyebrow, pox-marked rough face, walks in. Men clear a path. A guy mid-set on preacher curls drops a forty-pound bar and offers the bench.

(SOUND: iron plates clank, gym echo)

Nick nods, loads a fifty-pound bar for warm-ups. The younger brother—average working-class build, everyman face, quieter—watches from the side, holding the rest of the bags.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He had a pair on him the size of steel wrecking balls. People took notice.

EXT. ALLEY BEHIND KIRK STREET BUILDING - DAY (FLASHBACK, CHILDHOOD)

Dented garbage-can lids for bases. Kids play kickball. LITTLE PETER, about nine, rumpled jeans and tee. NICK (CHILD), already tall sixth-grader energy, devilish messy hair, bigger than peers.

(SOUND: ball thud, kids yelling, city alley echo)

A ball smashes through a kitchen window. Glass rains.

(SOUND: glass shatter)

MRS. ENGEL (O.S.)
Who did that?!

Little Peter freezes. Nick (child) steps up.

NICK (CHILD)
I did it.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He never told Ma it was really Mark. None of us did. Somehow a twelve-year-old came up with sixty bucks.

INT. KIRK STREET FIFTH-FLOOR APARTMENT MILWAUKEE - DAY

Worn living room. MA, aging, brown hair, tired saggy-puppy brown eyes, house dress and cardigan, afghan on the overstuffed brown chair, watches a game show. Pill bottles lined on the kitchen counter.

(SOUND: game-show applause from CRT TV, floorboards creak)

Nick, jeans, boots, brown suede jacket, tosses keys in the basket by the door. The younger brother—PETER in production terms, never named aloud yet—in pizza-work clothes, flour dust on a plain tee.

NICK
What's Ma doin' back in the bedroom at this time of day?

PETER
I don't know, why?

NICK
You got to keep an eye on her. She shouldn't be sleeping in the middle of the day like that. That's how old people go downhill.

Ma peels her eyes from the TV long enough for a vague smile.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Nick has a job down at the local metal factory. He runs one of the assembly lines. Sometimes the big crane. He must make a lot. Nice car. Cool apartment on Werner Street. Five blocks away. Feels like he still lives here.

EXT. STREET OUTSIDE PIZZA PARLOR - DAY

Burnt-orange 1980 Pontiac Trans Am with yellow flames and hood eagle idles at a light. People stare. Nick at the wheel.

(SOUND: V8 rumble, light city traffic)

Peter locks a bike to a rack, plain work clothes, heads for the parlor door.

INT. PIZZA PARLOR - DAY

Warm oven glow. Phone, peels, boxes. TONY, middle-aged owner, practical polo, kind eyes, works the counter.

(SOUND: oven doors, dough slap, phone ring)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I can't afford to go back to school. Somebody has to take care of Ma. Nick says you don't need a degree. All you need is to know somebody.

EXT. PIZZA PARLOR SIDEWALK - DAY

A girl—SIONNA, pale English-story skin, short brown hair, lightest watery blue eyes, black short-sleeved V-neck, jeans, blue tennis shoes, big backpack, fish-symbol necklace—trips on the cement and brushes Peter's shoulder.

(SOUND: shoe scuff, soft impact, street ambience)

PETER
Are you okay?

SIONNA
Yes.

She brushes off her shirt. They look at each other.

PETER
I work here. I'm just going to work. What is your name?

SIONNA
Sionna.

PETER
Sionna? That's different. What does that mean?

SIONNA
Mom and dad named me after an Irish goddess. Something about the River Shannon.

He is not really listening. Those eyes—pre-dawn sky blue.

PETER
You want to come in and have some pizza?

SIONNA
No thanks. I don't eat pizza. But if you want to go have a cup of coffee sometime, you can call me.

She gives a number. Walks away, looks back over her shoulder. Smooth, boneless, like waves.

(SOUND: her footsteps recede, parlor door chime)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was like I knew her. Intense. She was washing up on me, and I didn't want to move my feet.

INT. KIRK STREET FIFTH-FLOOR APARTMENT MILWAUKEE - LATE AFTERNOON

Ma at the counter, glassy empty eyes, mumbling, searching among the lined pill bottles.

(SOUND: bottles rattle, TV murmur)

MA
I can't find my pain pills. Did we go to church today? Did you see Mrs. Canasta there?

PETER
Ma, what are you talking about? We don't go to church anymore. Mrs. Canasta lives down one level. Your pills are right in front of you.

She snaps back, soft apology. Peter grabs a jacket.

EXT. MILWAUKEE STREETS AND CITY BUS - NIGHT

Peter walks fast in the cold, past Joe's Bar glow, then crosses toward the public library, open till nine.

(SOUND: cold wind, distant bar music, his quick footsteps)

INT. PUBLIC LIBRARY - NIGHT

Big windows, wood floors, old lights on metal chains. Classic literature stacks. Peter at the desk.

(SOUND: quiet page turns, distant chair scrape)

LIBRARIAN
Do you have a library card?

PETER
No. How do I get one of those?

He fills a form. Temporary paper card. He pulls Wuthering Heights by Emily Brontë.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Who needed school when you could get the same thing for free? I started reading that night. Heathcliff and Catherine. Somehow in my dream I mixed up Sionna and Catherine.

INT. JAVA HUT COFFEE SHOP - DAY

College crowd, backpacks, textbooks. Sionna at a small back-wall table in washed-out jeans, soft leather shoes, long-sleeved loose green hooded top. Watery blue eyes catch light.

(SOUND: espresso machine hiss, low student chatter)

Peter sits, self-conscious among the cool kids.

SIONNA
What are you reading right now?

PETER
Wuthering Heights. This guy and girl grew apart but were really in love.

SIONNA
Yeah, I know. I read it.

She talks symbols—ghosts, double characters, the moors. Wild, unpredictable, infertile. A love that never changes destroys you.

(SOUND: cup set down, chair creak)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Something inside me was coming awake. She made me feel smarter just by being around her. We made another date for Friday night.

INT. DREAM - MEDIEVAL FOREST AND FIELD - DAY

Lush deciduous forest, moss, trillium, bleeding hearts. Peter in white ballooning sleeves, red embroidered tunic, brown hose, rides close behind a woman with long brown hair on a dappled mare—Sionna's face, light blue eyes. Rose and warm-grass scent of her neck.

(SOUND: hoof soft on forest floor, insects, then open-field hush)

They emerge into sunlit grass. He helps her down. First kiss. Palpable charge.

DREAM-SIONNA
(no words—only the kiss)

Peter bolts—

INT. KIRK STREET FIFTH-FLOOR APARTMENT MILWAUKEE - NIGHT

Peter sits upright in bed, sweating, chest pounding.

(SOUND: his hard breathing, night apartment quiet)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
What the hell was that? The clothes. Old kings. I knew that girl. It was Sionna.

INT. YOGA STUDIO (MILWAUKEE) - DAY

Purple mats. About twenty people. Peter in t-shirt and shorts; shirt falls over his face in a fold; he tucks it into his pants. Sionna beside him, calm. He struggles, out of breath, dizzy, groin stretch, aching back—then after savasana, deep calm.

(SOUND: instructor's soft counts, bodies shifting, collective exhale)

SIONNA
You were really great at that.

PETER
Are you kidding me?

SIONNA
No. You caught on very quickly. Your practice is your practice. The only thing that matters is what's going on on your mat.

INT. KIRK STREET FIFTH-FLOOR APARTMENT MILWAUKEE - NIGHT

Nick stomps, face beet-red, fists clenching. Ma sits small in her chair.

(SOUND: door slam potential, floor thuds, TV low)

NICK
Ma missed her doctor's appointment yesterday, you ass. Where the hell were you? What were you doing that was so important?

PETER
I totally forgot. I'm sorry. I was with someone.

NICK
With someone? With who?

PETER
I met a girl. We had a couple dates. That's all.

NICK
Oh, great. A girl. Don't you think Ma deserves better than that? Try looking at your priorities, Bro.

Nick slams out.

(SOUND: door slam hard)

INT. DOCTOR'S OFFICE / THEN HOSPITAL ER - DAY

DR. CARSON, white coat, serious eye contact, notepad.

(SOUND: clinic ambient, then ER monitors beeping)

DR. CARSON
Your mother's blood pressure is dangerously high. Risk of a stroke. Take her directly to the emergency room. We admit her for observation.

Peter on a pay phone, then Nick arrives furious. Ma holds her chest, ringing in her ears.

MA
Someone must be talking about me.

INT. HOSPITAL / ER / ICU - NIGHT

Peter asleep in the chair by Ma's bed. Dream bleeds in:

INT. DREAM - BATTLEFIELD - NIGHT

Chaos, blood, shining steel. A dying man bubbles blood.

DYING MAN
Save her.

Nick runs toward Peter—then stabs the longest knife into Peter's chest.

(SOUND: battle roar, then wet stab, Peter's gasp)

INT. HOSPITAL / ER / ICU - NIGHT

Peter wakes gasping. Ma dozes. Betrayal sits on his face.

(SOUND: hospital monitors, his ragged breath)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Nick had killed me. It took fifteen minutes to tell myself it was only a dream.

INT. KIRK STREET FIFTH-FLOOR APARTMENT MILWAUKEE - NIGHT

Home again. Blue weekly pill organizer on the counter. Nick in big boots and suede jacket, hair helter-skelter, pox-marked face like he broke out of prison. Mocks the organizer.

(SOUND: boots on floor, TV cop show)

NICK
Oh, holy shit! Aren't you nurse Nancy! How did you come up with this little idea?

PETER
Sionna helped me. She's really smart.

NICK
This chick sure has you wrapped around her finger.

PETER
Stop it, Nick! You haven't even met her.

NICK
Don't you talk to me like that, you little bastard.

PETER
I'm not a bastard!

NICK
You sure are a giant pussy—and that makes you and me way different, Bro.

PETER
You're so Goddamn hateful. What the fuck got into you? Was Ma on crack when she was pregnant with you?

Nick's hand WHACKS Peter's head. Ear rings. Nick stomps out, slams the door.

(SOUND: slap, door slam, Peter's stunned breathing)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
In that split second I knew I never wanted Sionna to meet that prick.

EXT. GRADE SCHOOL YARD / MRS STARK ERA - DAY (FLASHBACK)

Late spring. Lilacs. PETER (CHILD) and BOBBY SWENSON doubled over laughing. NICK (CHILD), big bad-assed sixth-grader, hair devilish mess, approaches. MRS. STARK strides up, glasses on beaded necklace.

(SOUND: recess kids, then stern adult heels)

MRS. STARK
Mr. Olsen! Would you like to explain yourself before I send you to Mr. Pauls?

NICK (CHILD)
He didn't mean anything. I'll talk to him. Ma will talk to him. He'll never act up again. I guarantee it.

Mrs. Stark softens for Nick, warns the little brother, marches off.

NICK (CHILD)
(later, mussing Peter's hair)
I'll always take care of you. Don't forget that.

PETER (CHILD)
I won't, Nick. I won't.

INT. KIRK STREET FIFTH-FLOOR APARTMENT MILWAUKEE - NIGHT

Months later feel. Peter in slightly tidier college layers, backpack. Nick visits, lips pursed.

(SOUND: keys in basket, TV)

PETER
I'm thinking about going to school. Community college. Bachelor's in business. Maybe manage a restaurant.

NICK
How the hell are you going to pay for that? And what for? This chick telling you what to do?

PETER
Sionna told me I could qualify for a low-interest loan. This was my idea. It would mean a lot if you could be on my side for once.

NICK
Bros before ho's. You get me? Nothin' is ever gonna come between us. Not school, not Ma, and not this girl either.

Peter forces a smile.

PETER
Sure, Nick. I get you.

INT. JOE'S BAR AND GRILL - NIGHT

Packed Saturday. Pool room left, bar right. Smoke, AC/DC. NICK in bar-night jacket, Jack and Coke. BOB and SAMMY stoned hangers-on. Waitresses in jeans and white blouses. LINDSEY with a dark-haired new boyfriend in the pool room.

(SOUND: rock music, pool balls, crowd roar)

Nick confronts them. The boyfriend bristles.

BOYFRIEND
You got a problem with me? You're in my face like a little bitch!

Nick's nostrils flare, fists clench. Peter steps in front of him.

PETER
Nick, listen—you don't have any problem with this guy. Lindsey can see whoever she wants. Leave it.

NICK
Keep out of it! You're going to be next if you don't get out of my face.

Slow motion: Nick's right fist draws back toward Peter's face. JOE and a BOUNCER rush in. Head-butt, scramble. Peter runs.

(SOUND: music blare, shout, fist whoosh cut by chaos, then Peter's sprinting breath)

EXT. MILWAUKEE STREETS AND CITY BUS - NIGHT

Peter runs like a bear is chasing him, chest exploding, to Sionna's duplex.

(SOUND: cold air in lungs, heartbeat in ears, shoes on pavement)

INT. SIONNA DUPLEX - NIGHT

Green suede sofa. Peter collapses into her. She strokes his face. Gray cat watches.

(SOUND: his broken breathing, soft house quiet, distant street)

SIONNA
Your brother tried to hit you. No one who really cares about you does that. He is a controlling ass. You are becoming a bravely compassionate being.

PETER
My purpose… what is my purpose?

SIONNA
I suppose we won't know until life is over. The purpose of your life is to find the true self within you, and to honor it.

She draws him down the hall. They peel clothes, climb into crisp fragrant sheets.

INT. DREAM - LAKE ANGEL - NIGHT

Black and white: muddy tunic, reeds, lake. Sionna rises from the water in white blowing gown, conductor arms, dry. Color surges—greens, blues, yellows. Embrace. Then NICK as giant from the woods, mace whirling, fire eyes. She spreads sleeves; invisible force pushes him back until he shrinks and vanishes.

(SOUND: wind howl, water lap, mace chain rattle, then sudden still)

INT. SIONNA DUPLEX - NIGHT

3 a.m. kitchen clock. They jolt awake on the couch, then shuffle to bed again.

(SOUND: clock tick, soft sheets)

SIONNA
Well, that sounds about right.

INT. KIRK STREET FIFTH-FLOOR APARTMENT MILWAUKEE - DAY

Months montage feel in one space: Sionna cooks butternut squash casserole; reads to Ma under the afghan; helps fill the blue pill box. Ma lights up for her. Nick absent. Peter exhausted between school, work, care.

(SOUND: oven timer, soft reading voice, pill plastic clicks)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Ma started to see me as the leader of our little family. Sionna was as much a help to me as to Ma.

EXT. STREET / INT. CAR (NICK'S) - DAY

Nick, already drinking, three Jack and Cokes in him, loads Ma in modest church coat into the car. He drives.

(SOUND: engine, ice in a glass earlier, tires on wet road)

Impact—metal scream. Cut before gore: silence, then sirens far off.

(SOUND: crash impact, then ringing silence, distant siren)

INT. KIRK STREET FIFTH-FLOOR APARTMENT MILWAUKEE - DAY

Empty Ma chair. Afghan. Peter stares. Cops' words still in his ears—toxicology pending, maybe drunk.

(SOUND: apartment hollow quiet, his broken sob)

PETER
Die you fucking jackass. Die. Die.

He bawls like a three-year-old, pulling his own hair. Sionna arrives, holds him.

SIONNA
Just let it out. We'll figure it all out.

INT. FUNERAL HOME AND CHURCH MASS - DAY

Old guy in a bad suit. Modest maple casket chosen. FATHER JOSEPH in clericals. Sparse attendees—apartment neighbors, old coworkers, BOB solemn. Rosary Society luncheon. Graveside at St. Mary's: Peter throws a handful of dirt. Muted thunk on the lid.

(SOUND: soft organ, dirt on wood, wind in cemetery trees)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I would be the only son there. The oldest one would not attend his own mother's funeral. Rightly so.

INT. HOSPITAL / ER / ICU - DAY

Five days later. Nick: sallow yellow, black-purple-green bruises, oxygen tube, IV, chest tube to a plastic box, right leg huge under bedspread, fire-colored hair sticking up. Weak voice.

(SOUND: monitors, oxygen hiss)

NICK
Hey, bro. I guess I really fucked up. Can't believe Ma's gone.

Tears. He talks Jack and Cokes, can't remember what hit them, vehicular manslaughter fear, lawyer, disability.

PETER
Whose fault was it, Nick?

NICK
You think I meant to kill her? You son of a bitch.

Peter looks at the trapped bulk—tubes, bed—and turns.

PETER
Think about it, Nick.

He walks out.

EXT. CATHOLIC CEMETERY ST. MARY'S - DAY

Hilltop flat stone near trees. Late fall sun. Marker: OLIVIA ANNE OLSEN. August 28, 1933 – September 7, 1998. 2 Timothy 4:7. Peter places golden mums in the inverted metal vase from a water bottle.

(SOUND: light wind, distant traffic, his quiet breath)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I have fought the good fight. I have finished the race. I have kept the faith. Yes, you did, Ma.

INT. PIZZA PARLOR - NIGHT

Tony throws Chevy keys. Peter delivers faster than TOMMY. Tony hits his shoulder—more deliveries, worry about how tired he looks, substitute-son warmth.

(SOUND: oven roar, phone, door chime)

Dream flash later in bed beside Sionna: farm, pregnant Sionna, newborn with a pizza for a face.

SIONNA
(half-asleep laugh)
It means you work too much. It also means you are giving birth to a new idea.

INT. PIZZA PARLOR - DAY

BOB enters, pupils huge, animated. Gray winter beyond the glass.

(SOUND: parlor clatter, Bob's loud man-man cadence)

BOB
Man, what the hell is going on with Nick? Cops hounding him. Might lock him up.

PETER
I'm not okay. I'm not okay with Nick. I'm not okay with Ma being dead. I'm not okay with anything.

Bob backs off, hands up. That night Sionna massages Peter's neck.

SIONNA
Someone once said not to trip over things behind you. This is your life. I love you.

INT. WERNER STREET NICK APARTMENT / LAWYER OFFICES / COURTROOM - DAY (NICK POV STRETCH)

Nick, bitter, hospital-weak then court-bound. Rants alone, then with MARK JOWLISH—greasy hair, shabby suit, cluttered desk—who passes him off. Then DANIEL DAWSON, nice suit, shined shoes, still-blond ~60. Court: fat lady judge, brown hair, big glasses. Involuntary manslaughter, jury ahead. Nick in black pinstripe.

(SOUND: bus air brakes, courtroom murmur, gavel wood)

NICK (V.O.)
That little bitch. It's all that little bitch's fault. He had one fucking job: take care of Ma.

INT. JUNIOR HIGH CAFETERIA / GOALPOST - DAY (NICK FLASHBACK)

Young Nick threatens curly-blonde JOHN JOHANSSON: hang upside down from the goalpost until you pass out and die. Later, childhood sidewalk: DAD in blue polyester puffy coat drags young Nick home, belt snaps in the bedroom. Welts.

(SOUND: cafeteria roar, belt crack, child stifled cry)

NICK (V.O.)
What comes around goes around.

EXT. MILWAUKEE STREETS AND CITY BUS - DAY

Nick on freezing bus route, heavy coat, among bag ladies and kids with green hair. Gray dead leaves. Walks to Sionna duplex. Dead summer pots on porch.

(SOUND: bus kneel, cold wind, his heavy boots on porch)

INT. SIONNA DUPLEX - DAY

Sionna opens a crack—loose hippie-leaning clothes, sandals, bracelets, dish rag. Gray cat. Flower candle, greens-and-beans smell.

(SOUND: door chain, cat soft mew, distant kitchen simmer)

NICK
You're the reason I'm probably going to jail. If it wasn't for you messing around with my brother, Ma would be alive.

SIONNA
Let's talk about this rationally.

He backs her to the hallway wall, left hand on her neck. Switchblade flicks open—Swiss Army. Edge between collarbone and breast.

(SOUND: her choked squeak, knife snick)

NICK
Break it off with him tonight. Cash in one week. Leak one word and I have friends who don't give a shit if your parents are alive or dead.

Front door THUNKS the wall. Peter bursts in.

PETER
Holy shit, Nick, what the fuck are you doing?

Struggle. Knife sinks. Red flies. Sionna makes weird noises, sinks. Nick elbows Peter, wipes blade on her shirt, flees.

(SOUND: door bang, scuffle, wet knife sound, her gasp, boots running)

EXT. MILWAUKEE STREETS AND CITY BUS - NIGHT

Nick on the bus, right hand bloody in pocket, left pays fare. Dark.

(SOUND: bus interior hum, his hard breath)

INT. SIONNA DUPLEX - NIGHT

Peter rips flannel, stacks hands on wound near left shoulder, blood soaking his shirt, hands, pants. Dials 911. Paramedics, defibrillator box, cops. He gives name: Sionna McAllister. Brother Nick Olson.

(SOUND: his 911 voice shaking, sirens approach, monitor beep—heartbeat still there)

PARAMEDIC
What happened?

PETER
My brother stabbed her. He stabbed her. Oh God, will she die?

INT. HOSPITAL / ER / ICU - NIGHT

Wait. Parents credit him for pressure that saved her. Shoulder injury, punctured lung. Nick sentenced later—attempted murder plus the drunk-driving death. Years.

(SOUND: waiting-room fluorescent buzz, distant codes)

INT. SIONNA DUPLEX - DAY

February Sunday. Months later. Sionna blank, zombie stare, loose recovery top for foam pillow under left arm. Narcotic flatness. Eyes without shine. Peter on the couch opposite.

(SOUND: house quiet, chair rock soft)

PETER
Are you depressed? What are you thinking?

SIONNA
I can't feel me. I can't feel myself anymore. Maybe we just need a break. Go to Mom and Dad's for a while.

PETER
Stay. Stay here with me and we can work things out. Please?

She walks to the bedroom. He leaves for work early—free fall.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
My fingers were slipping off the rocky cliff. I had no one now.

EXT. SEATTLE BELLTOWN / PIKE PLACE MARKET / LOWELL'S - DAY

Seven years later. Gray blanket sky. PETER now broader shoulders, arm muscle, Pacific-Northwest casual—quality jeans, layered knit, waterproof shoes. Walks past keyboard player in felt hat. Lowell's window: ferry on Puget Sound, cruise ship. Cinnamon roll like a halo. Flash of dark brown hair in crowd—not her.

(SOUND: market crowd, gulls, ferry horn distant)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I now had my own chain. Tony's Boy's Pizza. Seven years of loneliness and hatred for my brother.

INT. SEATTLE YOGA / MEDITATION GYM - NIGHT

Candles, citrus and lavender. Ten or twelve people. Gratefulness theme. Peter on a mat.

(SOUND: soft chant, bodies settling, rain on roof)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I can't help the void left by Sionna, and the resentfulness toward my own brother. And yet sorrow for him. DNA demands.

INT. SEATTLE BELLTOWN APARTMENT - NIGHT

Warm. Cat BOZO, orange and white, on the couch. Peter reads, then sleeps.

INT. DREAM - SAGE ROLLING-HILLS ROAD - DAY

Winding dirt road, bright green hills. Indian sage, dark skin, white flowing wrap. Dirty pizza box on the path.

(SOUND: wind in grass, footsteps)

SAGE
Stop judging things. All things are an illusion. You create your own reality. Start living the truth.

Sage and box vanish. Joy floods.

INT. SEATTLE BELLTOWN APARTMENT - NIGHT

Peter wakes smiling, writes in a notebook. Bozo against his stomach.

(SOUND: pen on paper, soft cat purr)

PETER
(to himself)
I have to see Nick. I have to forgive.

EXT. OLD COSMETOLOGY SCHOOL (FORMER PIZZA PARLOR) / ALLEY BEHIND KIRK STREET - DAY

Milwaukee return. Weeds. Sign: CENTRAL COSMETOLOGY—pink scissors, powder puff. Dark inside. Same lookout window. Alley: green and brown dumpsters. Hispanic couple with stroller at a dark brown door.

(SOUND: wind in weeds, distant traffic)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I had accepted the role of his serf. What a dupe I was. I was past it. A completely different person.

INT. MILWAUKEE DELI - DAY

Peter with Reuben, fries, iced tea. BOB aged—graying thinning hair, thicker waist, deeper lines—burger grease on his mouth. Bear hug. Patrons stare.

(SOUND: deli clatter, Bob's cackle, chair scrape)

BOB
Holy shit. You came back. You look so grown up. Big shoulders. You got out and did something. I'm so proud of you, man.

PETER
I'm here to visit Nick. I need to let the shit go.

BOB
I stopped going. Too sad. I took over his apartment. Factory job now.

EXT. SIONNA DUPLEX - DAY

Drive-by. Smaller, grayer. Naked porch—no chairs, no plants. Memory overlay: bamboo rockers, Smiley the cat, air kiss.

(SOUND: car idle, kids on bikes distant)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I loved her so much. I just want her to be happy. Leave her alone. Move on.

INT. GREEN BAY CORRECTIONAL INSTITUTE - DAY

Metal detectors, burly officers, double-locked doors. Waiting hour. Visitation: plexiglass, phones, yellow receiver. NICK opposite—scrawny, graying thinning hair, lined forehead, baggy sad watery eyes, orange institutional jumpsuit. Aged hard.

(SOUND: locks clank, muffled voices, phone plastic pickup)

NICK
Hi, bro. Glad you came. Looking pretty good for getting out early. Maybe two more years.

PETER
I'm here to tell you some things. Just listen. You're an asshole. You killed my mother drunk. You held me down my entire life. You tried to kill the love of my life and drove her away.

(SOUND: room tone, his controlled breath)

PETER (CONT'D)
And this is the most important bit. I forgive you. It sets me free. It releases your control over me forever.

Nick weeps. Tears hit Formica.

NICK
You're so right. I am a huge asshole. I feel so bad about Ma and Sionna. You're more than I ever was.

GUARD
Time's almost up.

PETER
I want you to get better. I still believe in you. After all is said and done, I still love you for being my brother.

NICK
What are you doing now?

PETER
I own a pizza chain in Seattle. You always said I couldn't do it. I did it, Nick.

NICK
President Olson. That's fucking amazing. I am so proud of you. I love you.

Guard ends it. Nick shuffled away. Peter sits with the yellow receiver.

(SOUND: phone hang-up click, door buzz)

EXT. AIRPORT DEPARTURE (MILWAUKEE/CHICAGO PATH) - DAY

Terminal bright. Boarding queue. Peter with tea and mixed nuts. Floating above the throng. Weight of thirty years gone.

(SOUND: terminal announcements, rollaboards, jet distant)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Somewhere she's out there. She's happy. I have to believe that. I send Nick love and hope. I will never see him again, as long as it's my choice.

Attendant scans the pass. Peter looks down at the name. Nick's proud voice in memory: President Olson.

PETER
(whisper)
That's me. President Peter Olson.

He walks the jet bridge.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
This has been Nick and me.

FADE OUT.

THE END